ifconfig # to view and configure network interfaces
ping # to check connectivity between two nodes --> It sends ICMP Echo Request packets and waits for ICMP Echo Reply responses
ping -c 5 google.com # Send specific number of packets
ping -i 2 google.com # Set interval between packets
  common usecase :- 
| Task                               | Example                 |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Check if a host is reachable       | `ping 8.8.8.8`          |
| Check DNS resolution               | `ping google.com`       |
| Measure latency                    | `ping -c 5 example.com` |
| Test internal network connectivity | `ping 192.168.1.1`      |

netstat #It displays active network connections, listening ports, routing tables, and interface statistics -->all active TCP connections.

| Command      | Description                                                  |
| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `netstat -a` | Show **all** active connections and listening ports          |
| `netstat -t` | Show only **TCP** connections                                |
| `netstat -u` | Show only **UDP** connections                                |
| `netstat -l` | Show only **listening** sockets                              |
| `netstat -n` | Show addresses **numerically** (no DNS resolution)           |
| `netstat -p` | Show **process ID (PID)** and program name using each socket |
| `netstat -r` | Display **routing table** (like `route -n`)                  |
| `netstat -i` | Show **interface statistics**                                |
| `netstat -s` | Show **network statistics** by protocol                      |

